.Im MicroEmacs command|macrodevelop
.so me.tni
.TH code-page-define 3
.XI code-page-define - "Define a named code page"
.SH NAME
code-page-define \- Define a named code page
.Me $a
.SH SYNOPSIS
.na
.I n
.B code-page-define
"\fIname\fR"
.ad
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B code-page-define
executes the macro file \fBcp\fR\fIname\fR\fB.emf\fR with the "\fC-\fR" and "\fC8859\fR" removed from \fIname\fR,
e.g. "\fCiso8859-5\fR" is defined by \fBcpiso5.emf\fR. The file defines the code page with
.Ht set-char-mask 2
flags \fBC\fR and \fBc\fR and makes it the current code page; in a build without Unicode support it also sets up the
display for it (flags \fBM\fR, \fBdp\fR and \fBa\fR,
.Ht $box-chars 5
and
.Ht $window-chars 5 ).
The user's display code page is set up this way when \*(mS starts.
.PP
With an argument \fIn\fR of \fC0\fR a code page not yet defined is defined but the current code page is left as it
was, this only gives the Unicode character of each byte, as needed to read a file or a spelling dictionary.
.PP
The code page file is executed with the same argument, a new code page file must follow this form:
.Me $emf-scheme
.CS
0 set-char-mask "C"
set-variable #l0 $result
set-char-mask "C" "\fIname\fR"
set-char-mask "c" "\fIunicode-table\fR"
!if &not @#
  set-char-mask "C" #l0
!elif &not &opt "uni"
  set-char-mask "M" "\fImapping\fR"
  .
  .
  set-variable .change-font.cp-box 1
!endif
.CE
.Me $ehf-scheme
where \fC.change-font.cp-box\fR is set when the file sets \fB$box-chars\fR and \fB$window-chars\fR, otherwise
\fBcodepage.emf\fR sets plain ones.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.na
.Ht set-char-mask 2 ,
.Ht $buffer-encoding 5 ,
.Ht change-buffer-encoding 3 .
.ad
.FH
